    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Did you have similar trouble paying attention or staying focused back when you were in elementary school?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, oh yeah. I was definitely like this as a kid. I mean, my mom used to say I had a motor attached to me. In elementary school, the teachers were always moving my desk around because I just couldn't stay put. I'd be up sharpening my pencil, or looking out the window, or... oh, man, I remember in third grade, Mrs. Gable's class, she put my desk right up against her desk so she could keep an eye on me. Mrs. Gable, she was this really strict older lady, always wore these bright floral dresses. She actually smelled like peppermints all the time. I think she kept a stash of them in her top drawer. I love peppermints, actually, the soft ones that melt in your mouth, not the hard ones that break your teeth. I cracked a molar on a hard candy once at a movie theater. It was that new action movie with the car chases, you know the one? The special effects were amazing, but anyway, the dental bill was ridiculous. \n\nBut yeah, back in school, before middle school even, I was always getting in trouble. I'd just blurt out the answers before anyone else could raise their hand, or I'd be leaning over into the next kid's desk, butting into their work because I was bored with mine. They just couldn't keep me in my seat.",
